  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday) Issued at 145 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nSaturday (Halloween) still looks like one of better overall\nweather days in the forecast period, as H25 heights actually rise\na bit in association with short-wave ridging, per the GFS, between\nthe departing short-wave mentioned above and the next significant\nPacific system taking shape offshore of CA. A few showers may\nlinger into the early afternoon in the northern mountains,\nhowever. Daytime temps look to stay within a few degrees F either\nside of normal.\n\nDeterministic guidance (GFS/ECMWF) have come into better agreement\non the initial phasing of the northern- and southern-stream\ntroughs. As the system comes ashore, a Pacific moisture tap will\nbe well-established. By 00Z Monday, a 110KT H25 jet is progged to\nlie across the Four Corners area, providing lift to precipitable\nwater values forecast to increase to ~0.8 inches in that area (and\nup to 1.1 inches in southern/SE NM). High (70-80%) PoPs re-enter\nwestern NM after 06Z Sunday, then spread westward through the day.\nExpect fairly long-duration light-moderate rain, with embedded\nshowers and a few thunderstorms. While there is still some\nquestion as to the degree of instability that could be tapped to\nsupport higher rainfall rates...even prolonged moderate rain could\ncause flooding impacts (both flash and eventually riverine) due \nto the very moist soils already in place, especially in NW NM. WPC\nhas internally introduced a higher-end Slight Risk (>25%) for \nExcessive Rainfall for this area on Sunday. At least moderate \nconfidence now that Flood Watches will eventually be required, at \nleast in western NM.\n\nMost of this initial round of precip will be triggered by the\nsouthern-stream trough, which opens up and moves on through NM\nSunday night, before the northern trough slows down and digs down\ninto northern/central NM on Monday. Snow levels, which have\ntrended up slightly for Sunday (now pushing 11,000 ft. MSL) fall\nto 8-9kft. Monday, though the deeper moisture will have departed\nby then. This would still yield a few inches of snow, mainly for\nthe northern mountains. Elsewhere, breezy and cool (temps 5-10 deg\nF below normal) with WNW winds on Monday and precip chances\ntapering off...lastly in NE NM. Gusty northerly winds hang on in\nthe Raton to Clayton vicinity for Tuesday. Ridging aloft builds\nupstream over the Intermountain West by Wed, for quieter weather\nlocally, though still a bit on the cool side.",
